    "narrative": "The lineup for LawDog today centers on **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (75.56) and **Jonathan Aranda** (69.13), our two strongest plays. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits from a favorable matchup against Grayson Rodriguez, where the +14% boost in SP skill outweighs the minor platoon drag, keeping his ceiling high in the #3 spot. **Jonathan Aranda** provides our best efficiency-to-matchup ratio, taking advantage of a positive park factor and a +12% dynamic platoon edge against Troy Melton to solidify his spot at 1B. While **Zack Gelof** (59.90) just missed the 60-point threshold for a \"Strong\" start, his +10% park factor and +7% platoon advantage against Connor Prielipp keep him in the mix, though he faces a slight penalty due to Prielipp\u2019s ability to neutralize base runners.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Luis Arraez**, whose score of 47.22 makes him a viable, if marginal, option. Ultimately, the optimizer prioritized the higher volatility and upside of **Gavin Sheets** (57.26) and **Daulton Varsho** (56.82); **Luis Arraez** was squeezed out largely due to a difficult matchup against Bryan Woo and a lack of significant stolen base upside, which is a premium factor for our squad. Similarly, **Justin Crawford** was monitored closely for his speed, but his 46.62 projection against Woo fell just short of displacing our existing outfield depth.\n\nRisk management remains a primary concern, as nearly our entire lineup is subject to \"Lineup Pending\" status, forcing us to rely on historical batting order assumptions. **Bryce Harper** (54.26) and **Jung Hoo Lee** (54.51) are particularly sensitive to these shifts; while **Jung Hoo Lee** gains a modest +2% boost from a slow-moving pitcher, any downward shift in the order could quickly push his daily efficiency below the 50-point floor. Meanwhile, **Cal Raleigh** faces the toughest individual matchup of the day against Jes\u00fas Luzardo (3.08 xERA), and we will need to monitor if his 48.43 score holds up should he be moved out of the #5 hole.",
